CORE no-new-smt
main.i
--arch x86_64 --floatbv
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
Exercises the conversion of an integer to x86 80-bit extended `long
double`.  Under the SMT2 back-end this is a floatbv_typecast whose
bit-vector lowering calls a `float_bv.floatbv_typecast_*` helper; that
helper has to be registered by `smt2_convt::find_symbols` even though
the operand is an integer (not a bit-vector-encoded float), which is the
gap closed by registering the helper for bit-vector-encoded *results*.

The test pins `--arch x86_64` so the x86 80-bit extended layout is
selected on every target, and deliberately calls no library function, so
CBMC never preprocesses the library for the x86_64 target.  It is
therefore fully host-independent (no x86_64-capable system preprocessor
needed) and is supplied pre-processed as `main.i`.

It runs under the SAT, cprover-smt2, --z3 and paths-lifo back-ends.  The
new SMT2 backend (--incremental-smt2-solver) is excluded via no-new-smt:
it exhausts memory bit-blasting the 128-bit x86-extended conversion.
